nuX_M0 provides GPU-accelerated ray-by-ray M0 neutrino transport on a
spherical grid. It consumes rate-independent leakage fields from
nuX_LeakageBase and returns electron-neutrino absorption and heating to the
Cartesian mesh.

At the beginning of each synchronized CarpetX coarse step, hydrodynamic and
metric fields are interpolated to the ray grid. The selected rate library is
then evaluated on the rays, the transport equations are advanced by the
elapsed synchronized time, and absorption is interpolated back to the
Cartesian mesh. nuX_M0 holds this nonlocal source fixed during the following
ODESolvers IMEX/subcycling step.

The radial transport sweep preserves the THC_M0 discretization and is
parallelized over rays on the GPU. `compute_every = N` executes on iterations
`1, 1+N, ...`. If M0 is switched off, Cartesian absorption is cleared at the
next all-level synchronization, independently of that cadence.

Each MPI rank currently owns the complete spherical grid and advances all
rays. Distributed ray ownership is intentionally deferred; the replicated
layout favors THC parity and simple interpolation but is not the final
scalability design. Energy-dependent opacity and ray excision are currently
rejected by parameter checks rather than silently approximated.
